# Read the whole GL client-array state at the play-screen crash, in one go.
#
#   $ lldb -p <pid>          (or let it run under lldb until it faults)
#   ... let the app crash: EXC_BAD_ACCESS at 0x0 in glDrawElements_IMM_Exec ...
#   (lldb) command source /path/to/rb458-crash.lldb
#
# Why this shape:
#
#   * Nothing here calls into the target. A jailbroken attach usually cannot install the expression
#     checkers, so anything that JITs code ("expr", and "p" on a call) fails outright. Every line
#     below is `frame variable`, which reads memory through debug info without running any code.
#   * No persistent ($) variables. LLDB keeps those for the whole session and refuses to redefine
#     one, so a second sourcing of any script would fail with "redefinition of persistent variable".
#   * No hardcoded struct offsets. The +0xNN comments in neGLES.h are the *original* binary's 32-bit
#     offsets; this 64-bit build lays the object out differently, so reading by offset would be
#     quietly wrong. Going through the type is the only correct way here.
#
# The fault is a null client array submitted through gleRunVertexSubmitImmediate. The instrumented
# build has already exonerated the element buffer and the vertex array, so the remaining candidates
# are the colour, texture-coordinate, weight, and matrix-index arrays -- all of which this dumps.
#
# If every line answers "no variable named ..." the build carries symbols but no debug info, and
# `frame variable` has nothing to read. That is a useful answer rather than a failure: say so, and
# the same state can be reached by symbol lookup plus offsets computed from this build's own layout
# instead of the type. Do not fall back to the +0xNN comments in neGLES.h for that -- they describe
# the original 32-bit object.
#
# The members below are private. That does not matter here: frame variable reads memory through the
# type, and access control is a compile-time rule with nothing to enforce at runtime.

# Select the frame of our own code rather than the driver frame the fault happened in. Walking the
# thread by name avoids the caller having to read the backtrace and pick a number by hand.
script
import lldb
thread = lldb.debugger.GetSelectedTarget().GetProcess().GetSelectedThread()
picked = None
for frame in thread:
    name = frame.GetFunctionName() or ''
    if 'EmitMatrixSprites' in name or 'RenderAxisAligned' in name:
        picked = frame.GetFrameID()
        break
if picked is None:
    print('!! no EmitMatrixSprites/RenderAxisAligned frame on this thread; run `bt` and look')
else:
    print('== selecting frame %d ==' % picked)
    lldb.debugger.HandleCommand('frame select %d' % picked)
quit

# The batch itself: sprite counts, buffers, and the scratch the client arrays point into.
frame variable *this

# The renderer's cached client-array state. m_aClientStateFlags says which arrays are enabled; a
# pointer that is null while its array is enabled and no buffer is bound is the fault.
frame variable *pRenderer

# The same fields individually, in case the aggregate dump is truncated or a member is optimised
# out. An enabled array whose pointer is null is the one to chase.
frame variable pRenderer->m_aClientStateFlags
frame variable pRenderer->m_nArrayBufferBound
frame variable pRenderer->m_nElementBufferBound
frame variable pRenderer->m_pVertexPointer
frame variable pRenderer->m_pColorPointer
frame variable pRenderer->m_apTexCoordPointer
frame variable pRenderer->m_pWeightPointer
frame variable pRenderer->m_pMatrixIndexPointer

# The per-array buffer bindings. Each Clear*Pointer only re-issues its GL call when the array buffer
# differs from the one recorded here, so a binding that disagrees with m_nArrayBufferBound means
# that array's pointer was never re-established for the currently bound buffer.
frame variable pRenderer->m_nVertexBufferBinding
frame variable pRenderer->m_nColorBufferBinding
frame variable pRenderer->m_nWeightBufferBinding
frame variable pRenderer->m_nMatrixIndexBufferBinding

# The batch's own buffers, for comparison against what the renderer thinks is bound.
frame variable this->m_dwIndexVbo
frame variable this->m_dwArrayVbo
frame variable this->m_pVertexScratch
frame variable this->m_nSpriteCount
